    record0.set("introduction", "A **Resume Builder** is your digital assistant for creating **professional resumes** that impress employers and pass through Applicant Tracking Systems (ATS). Whether you're a fresh graduate, experienced professional, or career changer, this tool simplifies resume creation with templates and guidance. Key benefits include: saving time with pre-designed formats, ensuring ATS compatibility, highlighting your strengths effectively, and maintaining consistent formatting. Job seekers worldwide use resume builders to create compelling documents that showcase their skills, experience, and achievements in a way that resonates with recruiters and hiring managers. Your resume is often your first impression with potential employers, making its quality crucial for landing interviews. A well-structured resume using a professional builder increases your chances of getting noticed by recruiters. The resume builder ensures your document is formatted correctly, making it easy for both human recruiters and automated systems to read. This is particularly important because many companies use ATS software to screen resumes before they reach human eyes. A resume that doesn't pass ATS screening never gets seen by recruiters, no matter how qualified you are. By using a resume builder, you ensure your resume is optimized for both ATS and human readers. The tool provides templates that are proven to work, saving you time and ensuring professional quality. Whether you're applying for your first job or your tenth, a resume builder helps you present your best self to potential employers.");
    record0.set("howToUse", "**Step-by-Step Guide to Building Your Resume:**\n\n**Step 1: Choose a Template**\nSelect a clean, professional template that matches your industry. Tech roles prefer modern designs, while corporate positions suit traditional formats. The template you choose sets the tone for your entire resume. A well-designed template makes your resume stand out while maintaining professionalism. Most resume builders offer multiple templates, so choose one that aligns with your industry and personal style. Tech companies often appreciate creative designs, while traditional industries prefer conservative formats.\n\n**Step 2: Fill Personal Information**\nAdd your name, phone number, email, and LinkedIn profile. Keep this section concise and professional. Use a professional email address (firstname.lastname@gmail.com is better than partygirl@gmail.com). Include your phone number so recruiters can contact you easily. Your LinkedIn profile link is valuable because it provides additional information about your professional background.\n\n**Step 3: Write a Professional Summary**\nCreate a 2-3 line summary highlighting your key strengths. Example: \"Results-driven IT professional with 5 years of experience in full-stack development and team leadership.\" This summary should immediately tell recruiters why you're a good fit. It's your elevator pitch on paper. Make it specific to the job you're applying for, highlighting relevant skills and achievements.\n\n**Step 4: List Work Experience**\nFor each role, include company name, position, dates, and 3-4 bullet points describing achievements. Use action verbs like \"Developed,\" \"Led,\" \"Increased.\" Focus on achievements rather than responsibilities. Instead of \"Responsible for managing projects,\" write \"Led 5 successful projects, delivering 20% ahead of schedule.\" Quantify your achievements whenever possible. Numbers make your impact tangible and memorable.\n\n**Step 5: Add Education**\nInclude degree, institution, graduation year, and relevant coursework or honors. If you're a recent graduate, include your GPA if it's above 3.5. List relevant coursework that relates to the job you're applying for. Include honors, scholarships, or academic achievements that demonstrate excellence.\n\n**Step 6: Highlight Skills**\nList technical and soft skills relevant to your target role. Prioritize skills mentioned in job descriptions. If the job posting mentions \"Python,\" make sure Python is in your skills section. Include both technical skills (programming languages, software) and soft skills (leadership, communication). Organize skills by category for clarity.\n\n**Step 7: Include Certifications**\nAdd relevant certifications, courses, or professional memberships that strengthen your profile. Include the issuing organization and date obtained. Certifications demonstrate your commitment to professional development. They're particularly valuable in fields like IT, finance, and project management.\n\n**Common Mistakes to Avoid:**\n\u2022 Using unprofessional email addresses\n\u2022 Including irrelevant information\n\u2022 Poor formatting or inconsistent fonts\n\u2022 Spelling and grammatical errors\n\u2022 Making the resume too long or cluttered");
    record0.set("realWorldExamples", "**Scenario 1: IT Professional**\nA software engineer with 5 years of experience uses the builder to highlight projects, technologies (Python, AWS, Docker), and achievements like \"Reduced application load time by 40%.\" The resume emphasizes technical skills and measurable impact. The engineer lists specific projects, technologies used, and quantifiable results. For example: \"Architected microservices infrastructure using Docker and Kubernetes, reducing deployment time from 2 hours to 15 minutes.\" This specific, quantified achievement is much more impressive than generic statements. The resume also includes relevant certifications like AWS Solutions Architect or Kubernetes Administrator.\n\n**Scenario 2: MBA Graduate's First Resume**\nA fresh MBA graduate with internship experience uses the builder to showcase academic projects, leadership roles in college, and internship achievements. The summary emphasizes eagerness to learn and strategic thinking. The resume highlights case competitions, consulting projects, and leadership positions in student organizations. For example: \"Led cross-functional team of 8 in semester-long consulting project for Fortune 500 company, presenting recommendations to C-suite executives.\" This demonstrates leadership and real-world business experience. The resume also includes relevant coursework like Finance, Strategy, and Business Analytics.\n\n**Scenario 3: Career Changer**\nA marketing professional transitioning to product management highlights transferable skills like data analysis, customer insights, and cross-functional collaboration. The resume bridges the gap between previous experience and new career goals. The professional emphasizes achievements that demonstrate product thinking: \"Analyzed customer data to identify market opportunity, leading to new product line generating \u20b95 crore in first-year revenue.\" This shows the ability to think strategically about products. The resume also includes relevant certifications or courses in product management.");
    record0.set("tipsAndTricks", "**Advanced Resume Building Tips:**\n\n**ATS Optimization:** Use standard fonts (Arial, Calibri), avoid graphics and tables, and include keywords from job descriptions. This ensures your resume passes automated screening systems. ATS software scans for specific keywords, so if the job posting mentions \"Project Management,\" include it in your resume. Use standard section headings like \"Experience,\" \"Education,\" \"Skills\" that ATS systems recognize. Avoid creative formatting that confuses ATS software.\n\n**Keyword Inclusion:** Mirror language from job postings. If they want \"Project Management,\" include it in your resume if you have that experience. Create a list of keywords from the job posting and naturally incorporate them into your resume. This increases the likelihood of your resume being selected by ATS. However, only include keywords that accurately describe your experience.\n\n**Industry-Specific Formatting:** Tech roles can be creative; corporate roles need traditional formats. Tailor your design to your industry. A software engineer's resume can include a GitHub link or portfolio website. A banker's resume should be more conservative. Research what's expected in your industry and format accordingly.\n\n**Quantify Achievements:** Replace \"Improved sales\" with \"Increased sales by 35% in Q3.\" Numbers make your impact tangible and memorable. Instead of \"Managed team,\" write \"Managed team of 10, improving productivity by 25%.\" Quantification makes your achievements credible and impressive. Use metrics like percentages, dollar amounts, time saved, or customers served.");
    record0.set("commonMistakes", "**Avoid These Resume Errors:**\n\n**Too Much Text:** Recruiters spend 6 seconds scanning resumes. Keep it concise\u2014one page for entry-level, two for experienced professionals. Use bullet points instead of paragraphs. Each bullet should be one or two lines maximum. White space is your friend; it makes the resume easier to read. Avoid dense paragraphs that overwhelm the reader.\n\n**Poor Formatting:** Inconsistent fonts, irregular spacing, or misaligned text looks unprofessional. Use the builder's formatting tools to maintain consistency. Stick to one or two fonts throughout. Use consistent spacing and alignment. Test your resume on different devices to ensure it looks good everywhere.\n\n**Irrelevant Information:** Don't include high school achievements if you're a college graduate. Focus on recent, relevant experience. Avoid personal information like age, marital status, or photo (unless specifically requested). Don't include hobbies unless they're relevant to the job. Keep the focus on professional achievements.\n\n**Spelling and Grammar Errors:** Proofread multiple times. Errors suggest carelessness and reduce your chances of getting interviews. Use spell-check tools and have someone else review your resume. A single typo can cost you an interview. Take time to ensure your resume is perfect before sending it.\n\n**Generic Objectives:** Avoid generic objective statements like \"Seeking a challenging position.\" Instead, use a professional summary that highlights your unique value. A good summary tells recruiters specifically what you bring to the table.");
    record0.set("relatedTools", [{"name": "Cover Letter Generator", "description": "Create compelling cover letters that complement your resume and explain why you're the perfect fit."}, {"name": "Portfolio Builder", "description": "Showcase your work samples, projects, and achievements with a professional online portfolio."}, {"name": "Interview Preparation Guide", "description": "Prepare for interviews with common questions, answers, and tips to boost your confidence."}, {"name": "Career Path Planner", "description": "Plan your career progression and identify skills needed for your next role."}, {"name": "Skills Assessment Tool", "description": "Evaluate your skills and identify gaps to improve your marketability."}]);
